LightHLS
========

LightHLS runs the project pass sequence and estimates the latency and
resource use of a C/C++ HLS design. The current executable is built and
tested with LLVM 18.

Build
-----

From the repository root:

    Tests/Light_HLS_Top/Build.sh \
        -DLLVM_DIR=/usr/local/lib/cmake/llvm \
        -DPolly_DIR=/usr/local/lib/cmake/polly

The executable is generated at:

    Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/Light_HLS_Top

Run
---

    Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/Light_HLS_Top \
        <source.c-or-cc> <top-function> [config-file] \
        --freq <frequency> [--board <board>] \
        [--synthesis <enable-or-disable>] [--output <directory>]

The config file is optional, but `--freq` is mandatory for every invocation.
Neither a config `clock` entry, `HI_CLOCK_PERIOD`, nor the historical 10 ns
default substitutes for this explicit command-line choice. Set
`HI_HLS_LIB_PATH` when the repository's `HLS_Data_Lib` directory cannot be
discovered from the source path.

Use `--freq 100MHz` to request a 100 MHz target, which corresponds to a 10 ns
clock period. `Hz`, `kHz`, `MHz`, and `GHz` units are accepted. The command-line
frequency has priority over the config file and `HI_CLOCK_PERIOD`; `--fre` is
rejected as an invalid spelling. Vivado
post-RTL synthesis is disabled when `--synthesis` is omitted; enable it with
`--synthesis enable`.

Every run creates a `reports` directory. Without `--output`, it is created in
the launch directory. With `--output <directory>`, reports are written under
`<directory>/reports`. Human-readable Vitis-style `.rpt` files coexist with
machine-readable JSON. All pass logs, rewritten `.cc` files, LLVM bitcode, and
disassembled IR are always placed in `reports/.lighthls`; no `DEBUG` argument
is needed.

Vivado outputs are grouped under `reports/vivado_report`. The primary readable
and machine-readable summaries are `vivado_report.rpt` and
`vivado_report.json`; raw utilization, timing, log, Tcl, metrics, and checkpoint
files remain in the same directory.

Vivado synthesis uses an out-of-context kernel flow with rebuilt hierarchy. On
Linux it uses one Vivado worker and pins the process to the first available CPU.
These settings work around reproducible Vivado 2025.2 U250 crashes on the local
Intel hybrid-core host and are recorded in the synthesis report.

FPGA board libraries
--------------------

Instruction timing and resource data are selected independently for each FPGA
board. The default is the legacy Artix-7 library (`artix7_a12t`, part
`xa7a12tcsg325-1q`). Select the Alveo U250 library with:

    Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/Light_HLS_Top \
        <source.c-or-cc> <top-function> --freq 100MHz --board u250

The board can also be selected with `HI_HLS_BOARD=u250` or by adding
`FPGA_board = u250` to the config file. Precedence is command line,
environment, config file, then the `boards/default` library. `HLS_lib_path`
may point either to the `HLS_Data_Lib` root or directly to one board library.
The selected board and resolved library path are printed at startup.

Board metadata and instruction tables live under `HLS_Data_Lib/boards`.
`HLS_Data_Lib/tools/generate_vitis_library.py` regenerates a board library
from Vitis HLS C-synthesis reports. Vitis project directories and reports are
work products and should be written outside the repository.

Source pragmas
--------------

LightHLS accepts a validated subset of these Vitis HLS pragmas directly in
the input source. As in Vitis HLS, place PIPELINE and UNROLL inside the body
of the target loop:

    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        #pragma HLS PIPELINE II=1
        #pragma HLS UNROLL factor=2
        A[i] = B[i];
    }

Omit `II` to request the smallest feasible II under the clock, memory-port,
and dependence constraints modeled by LightHLS:

    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        #pragma HLS PIPELINE
        A[i] = B[i];
    }

Omit `factor` to request a full unroll of a loop with a positive,
compile-time constant trip count:

    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
        #pragma HLS UNROLL
        A[i] = B[i];
    }

As in Vitis HLS, pipelining an outer loop fully unrolls every loop nested
below it. Each nested loop must therefore have a positive compile-time
constant trip count. LightHLS reports an error if a required full unroll
cannot be performed; successful outer-loop pipelining can substantially
increase resource use.

Place ARRAY_PARTITION within the function that defines the target array:

    #pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=A type=block factor=2 dim=1
    #pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=A type=cyclic factor=2 dim=1
    #pragma HLS ARRAY_PARTITION variable=A type=complete dim=1

The default partition type is `complete`, and the default dimension is 1.
Use `dim=0` to partition every dimension. A factor is required for block and
cyclic partitioning and must be omitted for complete partitioning.
LightHLS rejects an unknown variable, an out-of-range dimension, or a factor
larger than the selected dimension. Complete partitions are modeled as
register storage rather than BRAM.

Function-level DATAFLOW and FIFO STREAM channels are also supported. Place
DATAFLOW inside the top function, before its canonical sequence of task calls:

    #pragma HLS DATAFLOW
    load_stage(input, loaded);
    compute_stage(loaded, computed);
    store_stage(computed, output);

LightHLS requires at least two unconditional top-level task calls. Nested
loops in a DATAFLOW region must be contained in task functions. The DATAFLOW
II is estimated as the maximum task interval, and intermediate array or
stream channels are included in the resource estimate.

For stream-based tasks, include `<hls_stream.h>` and declare local
`hls::stream<T>` channels. LightHLS supplies a small compatibility header
for compilation and recognizes the Vitis HLS STREAM syntax:

    hls::stream<int> loaded;
    #pragma HLS STREAM variable=loaded depth=4 type=fifo

`depth` defaults to 2 and `type` defaults to `fifo`. Native integer,
floating-point, and fixed-width `<stdint.h>` element types are recognized
for resource estimation. Stream arrays with positive integer literal
dimensions are expanded into their individual FIFO instances for channel
and resource accounting. Non-literal stream-array dimensions are rejected
because their multiplicity cannot be determined reliably. The compatibility
class models the blocking
`read`, `write`, `operator<<`, and `operator>>` interfaces needed to build
estimator IR; it is not a software FIFO implementation for C simulation.

Loop-level DATAFLOW and `disable_start_propagation` are valid Vitis HLS
forms but are not modeled. STREAM `type=shared`, `type=unsync`, nonblocking
stream operations, and arbitrary user-defined stream element types are also
not modeled. These cases produce explicit errors rather than silently using
different semantics.

`off` and `off=true` disable PIPELINE or UNROLL on the containing loop.
`ARRAY_PARTITION off=true` disables the matching variable and dimension.
Source directives override matching directives from the optional config file.

PIPELINE `style=stp` is accepted. Function-level PIPELINE, `rewind`,
`style=flp`, and `style=frp` are not modeled. UNROLL `skip_exit_check` and
`region` are also not modeled. LightHLS reports these options as unsupported
instead of silently applying different semantics.

The RTL backend supports PIPELINE for canonical single-block loops with a
positive compile-time trip count and integer operations. In a nested loop nest,
PIPELINE may target the innermost loop, or it may target one outer loop while
all descendant loops are fully unrolled. Applying PIPELINE to more than one
loop in the same ancestor/descendant chain is rejected. The achieved II and its
memory or recurrence limiting factor are reported in
`reports/scheduling/pipeline_schedule.rpt` and `.json`.

Run the self-checking Icarus pipeline regression after building LightHLS:

    Tests/PipelineBackend/run_pipeline_tests.sh \
        Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/Light_HLS_Top

App test matrix
---------------

Run three pragma configurations for every case under `App`:

    python3 \
        Tests/LLVM_exp14_HI_WithDirectiveTimingResourceEvaluation/run_source_pragma_variants.py \
        --build Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build \
        --executable Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/Light_HLS_Top \
        --freq 100MHz \
        --output Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/all_app_source_pragma_variants \
        --timeout 120

The three generated variants are pipeline with block partitioning, unroll
with cyclic partitioning, and pipeline plus unroll with complete partitioning.
Cases requiring `ap_int` are reported as `UNSUPPORTED_AP_INT` because `ap_int`
support is outside the current scope. The CSV summary and per-run logs are
written below the selected output directory, which is ignored by Git.

Run the dedicated DATAFLOW/STREAM checks against the designs under
`App/light_hls_dataflow`:

    python3 Tests/Light_HLS_Top/run_dataflow_pragma_tests.py \
        --executable Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/Light_HLS_Top \
        --freq 100MHz \
        --output Tests/Light_HLS_Top/build/dataflow_pragma_tests \
        --timeout 180

This covers PIPO array channels, scalar and array FIFO STREAM pragmas, and a
deeper FIFO configuration. The dedicated DataflowBackend regression also
validates per-transaction rates, capacity, graph II, zero-token-cycle rejection,
repeated PIPO bank reuse, and self-checking generated RTL simulation.
